    <h1 class="poisson-title">THE POISSON DISTRIBUTION</h1>

    <p>Suppose that the number of occurrences of some event in a specified interval...</p>

    <p class="equation">$$P(X = x) = \frac{e^{-\lambda} \lambda^x}{x!}$$</p>

    <p>where $x$ is a non-negative integer.</p>

    <p>The mean and variance of the Poisson distribution are both equal to $\lambda$.</p>

    <p class="theorem-heading">Theorem 1.1</p>

    <p>If $X$ is a Poisson$(\lambda)$ random variable, then its moment generating function is...</p>

    <p class="proof-heading">Proof:</p>

    <p class="equation">$$E[e^{tX}] = \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} e^{tx} \frac{e^{-\lambda} \lambda^x}{x!} = e^{-\lambda} \sum_{x=0}^{\infty} \frac{(\lambda e^{t})^x}{x!} = e^{-\lambda} e^{\lambda e^{t}} = e^{\lambda(e^{t} - 1)} \ldots$$</p>

